WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:06.700
Ever since humans invented the wheel we have
been obsessed with speed.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.500
For thousands of years we have been designing
and engineering vehicles and techniques to

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.220
help us move faster.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.981
We eventually invented the car which allows
us to travel anywhere at speeds of up to a

00:00:17.981 --> 00:00:20.520
few hundred kilometres per hour.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.070
We then quickly realised that this was a grave
mistake because all it did was allow us to

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.760
get to work faster.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.990
So we invented even faster airplanes so we
can instead go sit on a sunny beach somewhere

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.760
sipping a Piña Colada.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.880
But this incessant pursuit of speed has come
with some strange side effects, namely the

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.070
sonic boom.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:46.969
At sea level sound travels at 340.29 m/s,
which is pretty darn fast.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:53.289
But in 1947 some bloke called Chuck Yeager,
who was an American test pilot and speed-obsessed

00:00:53.289 --> 00:01:02.059
lunatic, managed to travel at 428 m/s and
break the sound barrier for the very first

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:05.489
time, using a Bell X-1 aircraft.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:10.979
Witnesses heard an extremely loud boom and
saw something quite strange, a cone like object

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:13.220
emanating from the aircraft.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.300
This would come to be known as the sonic boom.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.270
But what is a sonic boom and what causes it?

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:26.420
Let's find out...

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.850
Sound travels as a wave, with crests and troughs.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.220
If you were to visualise sound waves they
would look like sphere's emanating from the

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:39.319
source of the sound, with each ring representing
a crest of the sound wave.

00:01:39.319 --> 00:01:45.300
The crests of sound waves appear almost perfectly
symmetrical when the object emitting the sound

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.750
is stationary.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.640
However when it starts to move something peculiar
happens.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.550
The spherical sound wave crests that are traveling
in the same direction as the object get squashed

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:02.450
together and each wave reaches the observer
quicker than the previous wave.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.390
Yeah I know, that can be a bit tricky to wrap
your head around, but basically, when an object

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:09.800
moves, sound gets squashed.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.970
The result of this sound wave compression
is an increase in the frequency of the sound.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:19.500
This means the sound becomes higher pitched,
as the object comes closer to you.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.340
This is why cars approaching you sound higher
pitched than when they're driving away from

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.340
you.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.040
Think of it like putting a big burly bloke
on a fast roller coaster, his voice may sound

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.030
deep and full of confidence when he straps
himself into that seat.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.730
But start that baby up and he starts squealing
like a little pig.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:42.350
Okay that's not totally caused by sound wave
compression, more a general dislike for roller

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.490
coasters, but you get the point.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:49.060
On the flip side however, wave crests that
are being emitted in the opposite direction

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:54.240
to which the object is traveling, take longer
to reach your ears, so you hear the noise

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.150
at a lower pitch.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.480
This is known as the Doppler Effect.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.290
But why on Earth am I telling you about all
of this?

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.590
How does it relate to a sonic boom?

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.540
Well, it's extremely important to understand
the doppler effect because a sonic boom is

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.570
created by exploiting the doppler effect.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.860
Take a high-speed aircraft for example.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.260
As it travels through the air it is constantly
emitting sound waves.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.350
Just like any other sound-emitting object,
these waves radiate from the source in a spherical

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.350
fashion.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.960
The sound coming from a jet is mostly a mix
of engine noise and air resistance as it pushes

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.340
air molecules out the way at very high speeds.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.319
The latter is what is responsible for that
"whooshing" sound you hear as an aircraft

00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:36.700
passes overhead.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:40.570
The wave crests being emitted in front of
the aircraft are being compressed.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:44.890
The faster the aircraft is travelling, the
closer together these waves get.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:51.580
But, as long as the vehicle's velocity is
not higher than 340.29 m/s, that's the speed

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:56.040
of sound, the waves will never touch each
other or overlap.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.930
Because each wave is traveling fast enough
to "get out of the way", before the next wave

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:01.930
is emitted.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:07.580
But what happens when you travel faster than
the speed of sound and don't allow each wave

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:12.110
enough time to propagate outwards before the
next wave is emitted?

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.850
This is where things start to get funky.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:20.030
When the aircraft exceeds the speed of sound,
each sound wave is released ahead of the previous

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:24.650
wave, because the object that is emitting
the waves is travelling faster than the waves

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.030
themselves.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:31.400
This causes the waves to get pushed together
and create a single sound wave with an enormous

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.490
amount of energy called a shock wave.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.020
If we could see this effect in real life,
the sound waves would appear as a cone trailing

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.900
behind the aircraft.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.930
This is known as a "Mach Cone".

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:48.590
In the field of aeronautics the speed at which
sound travels in known as "Mach One".

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:54.320
The result of this extreme compression of
sound waves is a very deep, audible boom,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.650
which we refer to as a sonic boom.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:02.530
Any observer watching the aircraft pass by
wouldn't actually hear the sonic boom until

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.810
the very tail of the Mach cone reaches their
ears.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.560
However, the pilot as well any passengers
aboard the aircraft, wouldn't hear the sonic

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.300
boom at all because they are travelling faster
than the actual sound of the boom, so the

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.600
sound will never catch up to the aircraft
and will never reach their ears.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.590
They can sit comfortably in their cabin sipping
latte's, whilst travelling faster than the

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:30.310
speed of sound in relative silence, unbeknown
to the fact that they're deafening every poor

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.800
sod that they pass below.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.419
Contrary to popular belief a sonic boom doesn't
just happen once, as an object breaks the

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:38.940
sound barrier.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:44.310
The Mach cone and the sound of the sonic boom,
are present for the entire time that the object

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.480
is travelling faster than the speed of sound.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.330
For as long as the aircraft is travelling
at this speed, the boom remains a constant

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.240
noise that follows the aircraft around.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:00.470
So that explains the sound, but what about
that visual effect of a sonic boom?

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:04.620
What causes that weird cone shaped object
to appear behind the aircraft when it breaks

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:05.990
the sound barrier?

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:11.420
This is caused by a rapid change in air-pressure,
which is caused by the high velocity of the

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:12.570
aircraft.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:17.600
This drop in air pressure causes the temperature
in the atmosphere behind the aircraft to drop

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.750
significantly, causing condensation.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:27.250
So what you're actually seeing is water vapour
in the air undergoing rapid condensation.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:31.900
This is not actually directly related to breaking
the sound barrier.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.960
Condensation clouds from aircraft can be seen
at a whole range of velocities.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.300
These condensation clouds don't always coincide
with a sonic boom.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.760
Whether you see this phenomenon or not depends
on many factors such as the humidity of the

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.600
atmosphere and the air temperature.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.889
It's not just high-speed aircraft that can
produce sonic booms though.

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:57.690
Sonic booms are found in many places in both
nature and the man-made world.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:02.380
When you crack a bullwhip, the cracking sound
is not caused by the whip hitting itself.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:07.810
The noise you hear is actually a tiny sonic
boom, created as the velocity of the whip

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.710
breaks the sound barrier.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.610
The pistol shrimp is also capable of creating
a sonic boom.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.580
This tiny little bugger is like the Chuck
Norris of the ocean.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:22.040
It can snap its claws together so fast that
it creates a sonic boom between its claws.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:27.970
This sound is so loud that it can stun or
even kill other sea creatures.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:31.430
That's basically like being able to kill someone
by clapping your hands.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.850
For goodness sake nature, calm down.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.980
That's just a ridiculous amount of power to
give to a shrimp.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:42.770
But it just goes to show, anything we can
do with technology, nature has probably already

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:46.479
beaten us to it.

